  "slug": "related-disorder-models-ad7b",
  "summary": "Two TTC5 studies and a dynein organoid study converge on mitotic and tubulin defects in cortical progenitors, alongside p53-dependent mesenchymal defects in a DBA model.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is disease-variant models that trace developmental malformation to progenitor-level cytoskeletal and mitotic defects. Ttc5 knockout mice show brain malformations from neurogenesis defects and mitotic errors with elevated γ-tubulin, extending tubulin autoregulation to TTC5. A mouse model of a patient TTC5 nonsense mutation reports impaired progenitor expansion, mitosis, tubulin homeostasis and neuronal arborization. Patient-derived cortical organoids with dynein variants show spindle disorganization and apoptosis, with differing impact across variants. Two items address related but distinct questions: RPS19 haploinsufficiency in mesenchymal progenitors causes p53-dependent growth plate defects independent of hematopoietic failure in a Diamond Blackfan anemia model, and Capsaspora shows a Hippo-dystrophin axis maintaining cortical actin. New here is the independent, convergent TTC5 evidence linking tubulin homeostasis to cortical development, and the variant-specific dynein organoid comparison.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
